On the Peter Atia drive, Atia breaks down how the liver keeps blood glucose stable through glycogen storage, release, and new glucose production.

81
00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:18.640
It's precise metabolic control, and understanding it helps explain how liver dysfunction can surface before obvious disease.

82
00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:36.000
Yeah, it sounds simple, but the precision required uh to regulate blood sugar is extraordinary.

83
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.760
Uh, and it never ceases to amaze me.

84
00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.640
It's one of my favorite things to explain to a patient.

85
00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:43.040
After a meal, glucose rises.

86
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:49.920
The pancreas releases insulin, and insulin tells the liver to absorb glucose and store it as glycogen.

87
00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:57.040
Now, when you've been fasting for some period of time and glucose dips, insulin falls, and then the liver does the opposite.

88
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:03.560
It breaks glycogen back down and releases glucose into the circulation.

89
00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:09.400
And when glucose runs really low, it can actually just manufacture glucose on its own.

90
00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:16.200
So if you're, you know, going more than a day without eating, the liver turns into a glucose-making organ.

91
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:24.600
If there's too much sugar to store as glycogen, the liver converts it into triglycerides, packages it into apalipoproteins, and ships them out.

92
00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.200
So what I want people to appreciate, though, is the scale of this.

93
00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:34.040
So everybody, you know, think about you go to the doctor, you get a blood draw, and it's, you know, it's a fasting blood draw, right?

94
00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.280
And you, you, you get back a number.

95
00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:41.160
And so let's say that number you get back says 90 milligrams per deciliter.

96
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.920
That was your blood glucose that morning when you showed up at the lab.

97
00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:53.480
If that's the case, your entire bloodstream in that moment contained only about four and a half grams of glucose.

98
00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:57.480
That's roughly a teaspoon, not a tablespoon, just a teaspoon.

99
00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:07.240
Yet a single meal, especially if it's a meal that I'm eating, may contain many times that, easily 90 grams of glucose, right?

100
00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:10.680
So 20 times that amount in one meal.

101
00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:18.120
And yet, despite that, a healthy person rarely moves more than a teaspoon above baseline, right?

102
00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:24.760
In fact, I'm trying to think of all the times I wore a continuous glucose monitor.

103
00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:40.200
If I ever saw a blood glucose lever that would have been north of about 160 milligrams per deciliter, someone with type 2 diabetes would rarely go above a teaspoon and a half at fasting.

104
00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:46.480
And when we can go hours without eating, our glucose still stays in that range.

105
00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:53.360
In fact, if you go days without eating, it might only dip to, say, 50 milligrams per deciliter.

106
00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:57.360
This is a monumental homeostatic achievement.

107
00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:10.880
And that reserve capacity to titrate out glucose in such fine amounts is exactly why early dysfunction is very easy to miss, which is something we'll, I'm sure, talk about.

